-# Refute 002: c0116
-- F1: fixed: the lock now detects a *change*, not only a live claim. `new raft`, `fold` and `recombine` record in the raft's `grouped:` line a hash of each prepared member's document as it is on `origin/main` when grouped (`raftplan.designHash`: the document without its `status:` and `raft:` lines, the two lines that ready, stop and the rafting PR itself change). The carried `status <R> prepared` and `ready <R>` refuse a member whose document no longer hashes to its record, naming it and both hashes ("<id> (<recorded> → <now>): changed on origin/main since <R> grouped it"). I chose recording over restamping: the user's plan says the raft stamp claims only the raft, and restamping would have brought back review-004 F1's give-back (`prepared>new`) for every release. Both reproduced orders are claims.spec cases: a redesign landed after the carried move, caught by `ready`; and one landed before it, refused by the carried move. The member then leaves through `unfold` (F3). Old-flow members have no record and are exempt (they carry the raft's stamp). A single-member raft is checked too. consequences: keeping a redesigned member means folding it again (a new record) and rerunning the coherence review (z-raft §5); the record is a new format, so it has a parser that refuses a malformed entry or an id twice (plan.spec); M69 to M73 run and killed.
-- F2: fixed: one shape, kept. A retired raft lives in `<root>/rafts/retired/<R-id>`. The full gate on a copy of today's tree with R0040 retired by `recombine R0030 R0040` (E3) turned exactly the three named specs red. Each is answered: `tests/lib/engine.luau`'s `engine.empty` clears `retired/` (which fixed c0005/worktree); c0114/classified is overridden (a retired raft is still filed in its root); c0043/rounds is overridden (its walk includes `retired/`). `metrics` now lists retired rafts in id order with the rest (c0043 pins one sorted list). The other walkers were re-checked: the store, the board, `sweepStale`, `migrate`, `paths`, `trail` and history read only the lifecycle folders, which is intended (history counts issues only, and trail has no capture for rafts); `audit`, `lessons` and `compact` read `retired/`. A new retired.spec runs c0114/classified, c0043/rounds, `lessons --check` and `audit` on a clone holding a retired raft, and checks that `engine.empty` clears `retired/`; AC2 runs it. consequences: c0005 needs a committed tree, so it is covered by the `engine.empty` case (its failure mechanism) and by E3's full gate, not by a nested run; M77 and M78 run and killed.
-- F3: fixed: `./zn unfold <R-id> <issue>...` claims the raft as `fold` does, empties each member's `raft:`, drops it from `issues:` and `grouped:`, and commits. It is the route back to z-design, during a rafting or after it landed. z-design's refusal names it ("z-raft takes it out first (./zn unfold <R> <id>)"), and z-raft §4's "recombine it into a raft of its own" is replaced. Spec cases: unfold in the redesign case, and on a landed raft (R0090), after which z-design's claim succeeds. consequences: a new command in every exhaustive list (zz-rules' table, the refusing list, Other commands, the usage); M74 and M75 run and killed.
-- F4: fixed: `release` also gives back the issue claims this checkout remembers for the raft's members, and the "claimed by another terminal" refusal names the recovery for a cleared `.cache` ("./zn status <id> new, then claim it again"). claims.spec case. consequences: none; M76 run and killed.
