+### 4. Adapters (`z-nile/engine/adapters.luau`, pure) and the runner (`runner.luau`)
+```lua
+export type Request = { prompt: string, model: string, effort: string?, cwd: string, session: string }
+export type Acc = { live: number, final: number?, byMessage: { [string]: number }, quota: string?, errorText: string?, lastText: string, session: string? }
+export type Adapter = { cli: string, runtime: string, estimated: boolean, argv: (Request) -> { string }, feed: (Acc, string) -> () }
+adapters.get(runtime: string): Adapter
+adapters.finish(acc: Acc, exitOk: boolean, stderr: string, runtime: string): ("ok" | "failed" | "quota", string)
+adapters.tokens(acc: Acc): number      -- final when the CLI gave one, else the running count
+adapters.QUOTA, adapters.WAITING, adapters.STDIN
+```
+| runtime | command line (probed 2026-10-21 where marked) | stream | running count | final count |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| `claude-cli` (E1 ✔) | `claude -p <prompt> --output-format stream-json --verbose --model <m> --permission-mode bypassPermissions --permission-prompts none --session-id <uuid> [--effort <e>]`, cwd = the worktree | NDJSON | each `assistant` message's `usage`, once per message id (its blocks repeat it) | `result.modelUsage`, summed over models (subagents included) |
+| `claude-cloud` (E6, unproven) | `claude --cloud <prompt> -p --output-format stream-json --verbose --model <m>` | as claude-cli | as claude-cli | as claude-cli |
+| `grok-cli` (E1 ✔, tools ✔) | `grok -p <prompt> --output-format streaming-json -m <m> --always-approve --cwd <wt> -s <uuid> [--reasoning-effort <e>]` | NDJSON (ACP updates) | each `usage` line (one per model call) | the `end` line's `usage` |
+| `grok-bot` (E7, unproven) | `grok agent -m <m> [--reasoning-effort <e>] --always-approve headless`, the prompt on stdin | as grok-cli | as grok-cli | as grok-cli |
+| `cursor-cli` (E1 ✔) | `agent -p --output-format stream-json --model <m>[\[effort=<e>\]] --force --trust --sandbox disabled --workspace <wt> <prompt>` | NDJSON | none until the end: an estimate, a token per four characters streamed (`estimated = true`) | the `result` line's `usage` |
+| `cursor-app` (E8, unproven) | `agent worker --model <m> --workspace <wt>`, the prompt on stdin | as cursor-cli | as cursor-cli | as cursor-cli |
+**The count**, the same for every CLI: input + cache writes + output (reasoning included in output by all three), cache reads left out (Guess G3). From the probes: claude 2 + 9,841 + 4 = 9,847; grok (16,114 + 112) + (296 + 11) = 16,410 + 123 = 16,533 (the per-call lines sum to the end line); agent 15,155 + 28 = 15,183. The caps (`max_tokens`) use the same count.
+
+**Quota** ("error only … make sure the cli doesn't hang in waiting for more quota"): the dispatcher moves down `order` only on outcome `quota`, which is: claude's `rate_limit_event` with `status = "rejected"` (it is printed once per call; the probe showed `allowed` with `utilization 0.78`), an error `result` or error line whose text names a rate or usage limit (`adapters.QUOTA`), a failed exit whose stderr names one, or a stall whose last words were a wait (`adapters.WAITING`: "retrying in", "resets at", …). No probe could make a CLI exhaust its quota without spending it (E4); the stall rule is what makes any CLI fail rather than wait.
+
+**The runner** (`runner.run(Request) -> Result`, outcomes `ok | failed | quota | cap | stalled | stopped`): the CLI starts under a perl launcher that calls `setpgrp`, writes its pid (= the group id) to `<state>/pid` and `exec`s the CLI, so one `kill -TERM -<pgid>` (then `-KILL` after 2 s) stops the CLI and everything it started (a `check.sh`, a Bash tool's children). stdin is closed (or carries the prompt for the stdin runtimes). It reads stdout line by line into the adapter, keeps the last 4 KB of stderr, and every 50 ms checks, in order: the stop marker (`stopped`), the running count past `max_tokens` (`cap`), a quota signal (`quota`), and the time since the last stdout line against `stall_seconds` (`quota` after a wait message, else `stalled`). A killed step's count is what it streamed so far. A process that left the group (setsid) is not waited for (zz-test, "Caught only by review").
+
+### 5. The dispatcher (`z-nile/engine/dispatcher.luau`; `cmd.dispatch` in `main.luau`)
+```
+dispatch <id> [--dry-run]                              the chain from the item's tracker state
+dispatch new "<description>" [--project <name>] [--dry-run]   z-ticket, then the chain on the new issue
+dispatch <step> <id> [--mode design|code|raft] [--dry-run]    one step
+dispatch stop|retry|status <id>
+```
+`main.luau` passes a context: `fail`, `mainRoot`, `onGitHub`, `checking` (below), `origin(id) -> { path, front, state, status }?` (origin/main's document, through `originTree`/`docPath`/`frontOf`/`stateOfPath`/`statusLib.effective`, the readers `ready` uses: lesson zz-arch/one-reader-per-format), `originIssueIds()`. `--dry-run` is a boolean flag (`flags()`); `dispatch` is not in `READ_ONLY` (it runs `guardRepoRoot`).
+
+**A model step** (`modelStep`): resolve the walk (§1); make or reuse the step's worktree with `./zn worktree <name>` (§2); for z-run read `./zn prompt <R> --path`; build the prompt (§3); then for each spec of `models.tries` in order: build the argv, resolve the binary on the caller's PATH (`command -v`, then its real path), refuse it when `checking()` and the real path is not under the outermost `.cache` this checkout lies in ("refusing to start <cli>: inside a check run only a CLI under this checkout's .cache may start (found <path>)": programme review F6), refuse a missing binary ("<cli> is not installed"; not quota, so the chain stops), start it through the runner with this environment, record the attempt, and continue only when the outcome is `quota`:
+- `PATH` = `<repo>/z-nile/bin:` + the caller's PATH (the wrapper, §6);
+- `ZN_DISPATCH=1`, `ZN_HEADLESS=1`, `ZN_DISPATCH_DIR` = the item's run-state folder, `ZN_DISPATCH_HOME` = `.cache/dispatch` of the main checkout, `ZN_REAL_GIT`, `ZN_REAL_GH` (the real binaries, resolved before the wrapper is prepended).
+
+`checking()` asks the guards' script like `guarded()` does: `z-nile/lib/fence.sh` gains `pickman_checking`, which prints `checking` inside a spec run or a fenced `check.sh` and `free` otherwise; only the exact `free` lets a CLI outside `.cache` start (c0114's "a guard's no is one explicit answer"). The engine names no fence variable (repo-fence's banned list).
+
+After the attempts: z-ticket's id is the one issue on `origin/main` that was not there before (anything else fails "z-ticket made <n> issues"); a review's note is the review file it wrote (`design-review-NNN`, `review-NNN`). **Spend** (`record`): each attempt that spent tokens gets one row with its model id and minutes: a `code-review` row is printed as `record: ./zn commit -m "<id>: <note>" --phase code-review --time <m> --tokens <n> --model <id>` for the author (it rides with the review file, as today); a `deliver` row, or any row for an item that is not in the backlog, goes to `pending.json`; every other row is `./zn spend <id> --phase <phase> --time <m> --tokens <n> --model <id> --note <note>` from the `zn-dispatch` worktree (synced first; `spend` changes nothing there and counts no prep lines from it). A failed publish keeps the row in `pending.json`. **Who records what** (programme review F8): the dispatcher records each dispatched session's tokens and model; a step under the dispatcher records its own minutes and lines as its skill says, without `--tokens` for its own session; a dispatched reviewer is a session of its own, so its row is the dispatcher's and nothing is counted twice.
+
+**A script step** (`scriptStep`): `./zn <command>` with forwarded output, in the main checkout (`z-ready`, `z-stop`, `zz-stats`) or the `raft-<id>` worktree; its outcome is its exit code.
+
+**The run state** (programme review F10, for c0118): `.cache/dispatch/<id>/state.json` = `{ id, mode ("chain"|"step"), step, reviewMode, cli, runtime, model, pgid, worktree, startedAt, tokens, outcome, detail, follows, history: [{ step, cli, runtime, model, outcome, detail, tokens, minutes }], pending: [{ id, phase, tokens, model, minutes, note }] }`, written through `sh.writeReplacing`; `questions.md` (from a step), `answered-<n>.md`, `pid`, `stop`; `.cache/dispatch/authorised/<pr>`. All ignored, in the main checkout (AGENTS.md's ignored-files list names `.cache/dispatch/`). `status <id>` prints it; `stop <id>` writes the stop marker and kills the group; `retry <id>` keeps `questions.md` as `answered-<n>.md`, clears the outcome and reruns the same step (a one-step run) or the chain, with the same order.
+
+### 6. The chain (`dispatch.next(Facts) -> Action`, pure; `dispatcher.facts` reads them)
+```lua
+export type Pr = { number: number, state: string, draft: boolean, green: boolean, mergeable: boolean }
+export type Facts = { id: string, kind: "issue" | "raft" | "none", where: string, status: string, design: string,
+	raft: string, ownClaim: boolean, localRaft: string, pr: Pr?, questions: boolean,
+	last: { step: string, outcome: string }?, uncovered: { string }, autoMerge: { string }, description: string? }
+export type Action = { kind: "step" | "merge" | "stop", step: string?, target: string?, gate: string?, pr: number?, why: string?, code: number? }
+dispatch.EXIT = { done = 0, failure = 1, user = 3, gate = 4 }
+```
+Facts come only from tracker state, never from a model's prose (F3): origin/main's document (`where`, effective `status`, `design:`, `raft:`), the run state (`last`, `ownClaim` = this dispatcher ran z-design on it, `questions`), the rafting worktree's `raft:` line (`localRaft`), `./zn lessons --uncovered <id>` in the last step's worktree, and on a GitHub origin `gh pr view <branch> --json number,state,isDraft,mergeable,statusCheckRollup` for the PR the item's state points at (`prep-<slug>` while preparing, `raft-<id>` while prepared and raftless, `<R>-<slug>` while active; `green` = every check SUCCESS, NEUTRAL or SKIPPED, and at least one).
+The rule, in order (each a row of the table):
+1. `questions.md` → stop 3, "needs the user" (F4; the scope escalation of the approved plan).
+2. The last step ended `failed`, `cap`, `stalled`, `stopped` or `quota` (every CLI) → stop 1, naming `./zn dispatch retry`.
+3. The last step was z-design, z-raft, z-run or zz-review and `lessons --uncovered` lists a finding → stop 1 (the approved plan: the chain never passes a review step with an unharvested finding).
+4. A step that ended ok but moved nothing → stop 1: z-design and the issue is still `new`; z-design and no prep PR; raft-plan and no `raft:`; z-ready and the raft not active; z-run and its PR still a draft.
+5. No item: with a description → z-ticket; else stop 1.
+6. An issue: done → stop 0; with a `raft:` on origin and prepared or active → follow the raft; `new`, or `preparing` by this dispatcher → the prep gate when its PR is open, else z-design; `preparing` by another terminal → stop 1; prepared and `design: ready` → the rafting gate when its PR is open, else `raft-plan` (script, in `raft-<id>`), then z-raft on the raft it named; anything else → stop 1.
+7. A raft: done → stop 0 after writing the pending rows (`spend <R> --phase deliver`); prepared in the backlog → `z-ready` (script, main checkout); other backlog states → stop 1; active → z-run until its PR is open and not a draft (`./zn done` ran), then the **run gate**.
+8. **Gates.** `prep` and `raft` are passed by the dispatcher itself only when `dispatch.auto_merge` names them and the PR is not a draft, green and mergeable: it writes `.cache/dispatch/authorised/<pr>` and runs `gh pr merge <pr> --squash` through `z-nile/bin/gh` (so its own merge passes the same rule), then `./zn sync`. Otherwise stop 4, naming the PR and why. The **run gate is always a human's** (stop 4, "ready to merge"): z-run's Phase 6 is skipped under the dispatcher (F3). After the human merges, `./zn dispatch <R>` writes the run's pending rows.
+After each step the dispatcher runs `./zn sync` in the main checkout. At most 30 actions per run. Guess G4: a dispatcher the user started on an item counts as the user's request for its capture's landing and its prep and rafting merges (zz-rules' "a prep the user didn't ask for is not merged without asking").
+
+### 7. The git and gh wrapper (`z-nile/bin/git`, `z-nile/bin/gh`, `z-nile/engine/wrapper.luau` pure, `wrap.luau` its entry)
+The shims run the real tool at once for everything but `git … push …` and `gh pr merge` / `gh api` (so the tracker's hundreds of git calls cost nothing); those go to `lune run z-nile/engine/wrap -- git|gh <args>`, which decides with `wrapper.decideGit` / `decideGh` and runs the real tool or exits 1. Without `ZN_REAL_GIT`/`ZN_REAL_GH` a shim exits 2 rather than guess.
+- **git push**: the destination of each refspec (`src:dst`, `+src:dst`, `src` = `src`, `HEAD` = the current branch, no refspec = the upstream or the current branch) that is `main` or `refs/heads/main` passes only when every path between the merge base with `refs/remotes/<remote>/main` and `src` is tracker state: under a root of **origin/main's** registry (`projects.at`, never the working tree's: lesson zz-arch/guards-trust-only-origin) or `z-nile/next`. That is exactly what `publish` (lease pushes of tracker documents) and `land` (capture documents) push, so the tracker keeps working under the wrapper. `--all`, `--mirror`, `--branches` and deleting `main` are refused.
+- **gh pr merge** passes only with a PR number the dispatcher authorised (`$ZN_DISPATCH_HOME/authorised/<n>`); `--admin` is always refused. **gh api** writes (`-X`/`--method` other than GET, `-f`, `-F`, `--input`) to an endpoint containing `/merge`, `/protection` or `/rulesets` are refused.
+The wrapper is bypassable (an absolute path, `command git`), as the user accepted ("Branch protection + wrapper"): branch protection is the server's half (§8).
+
+### 8. Branch protection on `main` (a step for the user; never run by an agent)
+Guess G5. A repository ruleset, so that a pull request and the green `gate` check (`.github/workflows/check.yml`, job `gate`) are required to merge into `main`, force pushes and deletion are blocked, and the repository admin role may bypass, because the tracker's own state is pushed straight to `main` (`publish`, `land`) with the user's credentials:
+```bash
+gh api --method POST repos/philipbergen/pickman/rulesets --input - <<'JSON'
+{
+  "name": "main: pull request and green gate",
+  "target": "branch",
+  "enforcement": "active",
+  "conditions": { "ref_name": { "include": ["~DEFAULT_BRANCH"], "exclude": [] } },
+  "rules": [
+    { "type": "deletion" },
+    { "type": "non_fast_forward" },
+    { "type": "pull_request", "parameters": { "required_approving_review_count": 0, "dismiss_stale_reviews_on_push": false,
+      "require_code_owner_review": false, "require_last_push_approval": false, "required_review_thread_resolution": false } },
+    { "type": "required_status_checks", "parameters": { "strict_required_status_checks_policy": false,
+      "required_status_checks": [ { "context": "gate" } ] } }
+  ],
+  "bypass_actors": [ { "actor_id": 5, "actor_type": "RepositoryRole", "bypass_mode": "always" } ]
+}
+JSON
+```
+The bypass also covers an agent that runs with the user's token, so the ruleset guards against other credentials and against a merge on red CI through the API without `--admin`; the wrapper and the skills guard the agent (E9 says what to verify after it is applied). zz-rules' Merging paragraph now says so.
